WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:01.080
So, who's ready?

00:00:01.080 --> 00:00:04.330
Welcome to the Refuge Program! Come on!

00:00:04.790 --> 00:00:05.920
Garrard: I'm Garrard Conley.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:09.050
I'm the author of "Boy Erased,"
now a major motion picture.

00:00:09.050 --> 00:00:11.400
"Boy Erased" is the story of my

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.500
going into conversion therapy

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.480
after being outed to my parents

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:18.710
and the psychological
torture that I endured

00:00:18.710 --> 00:00:19.610
while I was there.

00:00:19.610 --> 00:00:21.750
You cannot be born gay!

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.020
Garrard: Also, it's the
story, I think, of my mom

00:00:24.020 --> 00:00:27.510
who takes me out of
therapy and saves my life,

00:00:27.510 --> 00:00:30.670
and also has to go on her
own journey of acceptance.

00:00:30.670 --> 00:00:34.126
I'm just here to try to
stop conversion therapy

00:00:34.126 --> 00:00:38.170
because I wish I had done my
homework before I took him

00:00:38.170 --> 00:00:40.010
instead of after I took him,

00:00:40.010 --> 00:00:43.830
because it's a horrible thing
and we need to get it stopped.

00:00:43.830 --> 00:00:45.700
Conversion therapy's taken many forms.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:48.750
It's been around since the
19th century, actually.

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.480
In the 1950's and '60s,
there was a hospital called

00:00:51.480 --> 00:00:54.400
St. Elizabeths Hospital
outside of Washington, D.C.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:57.210
They performed lobotomies,
they performed electroshock

00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:59.740
therapy, they had people in straitjackets.

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:03.240
At this moment, 36 states do
not have bans on conversion

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:04.720
therapy for minors.

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:06.180
The bigger battle, however,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:08.700
is that any religious organization

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:12.740
can perform conversion therapy
and charge for it currently,

00:01:12.740 --> 00:01:14.004
no matter what state you're in.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.758
I mean, I had it pretty easy growing up.

00:01:16.758 --> 00:01:19.732
We moved to a place called
Cherokee Village, Arkansas.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.520
We'd always been very
much a church family.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.950
I'd always been told that
being LGBTQ was not right

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.218
and I don't think there
were any exceptions to that.

00:01:30.218 --> 00:01:32.950
I think that, as I grew older,
and I started to understand

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.650
myself a lot more and my
sexuality, it became obvious

00:01:35.650 --> 00:01:40.310
that I was on a crash course
with these two ideologies.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:44.570
Being married to a Baptist
pastor and being raised

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:46.922
the way I was, we believed
exactly what the Bible said

00:01:46.922 --> 00:01:50.730
about homosexuality, and
that's what I was taught

00:01:50.730 --> 00:01:52.070
and I just didn't question it

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:54.980
because I think you don't
tend to question things

00:01:54.980 --> 00:01:58.670
until it hits your family
or someone that you love.

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:02.380
I was actually in my
first semester of college.

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:06.510
Someone that I knew at the time raped me

00:02:06.510 --> 00:02:08.750
and he confessed to me
that he'd also raped

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:10.360
a 14-year-old boy.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:13.040
Right after he raped me,
he confessed this to me.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.840
And I told some friends what
he'd done and he found out

00:02:16.840 --> 00:02:19.410
that I'd told that. And
so, as a preemptive move,

00:02:19.410 --> 00:02:20.700
he called my parents,

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.340
called my mom specifically,
because he really wanted to

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:25.350
strike fear and it worked.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:29.040
And when I came home, Dad
took me into his bedroom

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:31.800
and said, like, "Can you
explain to me what's going on?"

00:02:31.800 --> 00:02:34.060
And so I told him, "I think I'm gay."

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.820
First of all, we took him to the doctor

00:02:35.820 --> 00:02:37.380
and had his hormones checked, you know?

00:02:37.380 --> 00:02:38.780
That's how naive we were.

00:02:38.780 --> 00:02:42.380
Then we called the largest
Southern Baptist church

00:02:42.380 --> 00:02:45.386
in Memphis and they said,
"Oh yes, you're so lucky."

00:02:45.386 --> 00:02:48.870
"We have a unit right here
in Memphis and it's called"

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:52.520
"Love in Action, and they
have an 84% cure rate."

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:54.340
And I'm thinking, wow, you know?

00:02:54.340 --> 00:02:57.350
I didn't even know what
conversion therapy was.

00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.370
We did one-on-one therapy
sessions after I was outed

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:01.870
for about six months.

00:03:01.870 --> 00:03:03.820
I went to a residential program.

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:06.136
The main activity that we did

00:03:06.136 --> 00:03:07.636
was called a "Moral Inventory,"

00:03:07.636 --> 00:03:10.180
and we were asked to write
down every sexual fantasy

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:13.150
or experience we'd ever had,
and we would have to say it

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:15.227
in front of everyone, like
share that with everyone

00:03:15.227 --> 00:03:16.224
in the group.

00:03:16.233 --> 00:03:18.380
And then after that, they
would give me Bible verses

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:20.760
to sort of combat those
thoughts that I'd had.

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:23.960
I knew that if I didn't do
this, I would lose my family,

00:03:23.960 --> 00:03:26.570
my faith, perhaps, my community.

00:03:26.570 --> 00:03:29.910
By the end of the second
week, I was asked to do this

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.740
exercise asking me to imagine
my father sitting there,

00:03:33.740 --> 00:03:36.560
and basically say that I hated him.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:40.340
And I remember thinking to
myself, these are Christians

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:43.550
who are saying that in order
to be cured, I have to hate

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:45.260
someone not love someone.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:46.930
And Christ is all about love.

00:03:46.930 --> 00:03:49.000
Interviewer: How much
were you paying for this?

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.940
Martha: It was like $3,200.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.960
For just a really short period of time.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:55.660
And then we also had to stay
in a hotel for two weeks.

00:03:55.660 --> 00:03:58.820
Garrard: But his quote for you,
for me going out for a year,

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.970
for doing years of
therapy, how much was that?

00:04:00.970 --> 00:04:03.540
Well, it was like $21,000.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:06.190
Now this may be the
toughest, but most rewarding

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:08.430
12 days that many of you will ever face.

00:04:08.430 --> 00:04:10.780
Martha: We later found
out that all the leaders,

00:04:10.780 --> 00:04:13.460
I mean none of them had more
than a high school education.

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:16.210
Within a week, they started
talking to me about,

00:04:16.210 --> 00:04:19.440
"Oh he's not following
the rules. He's not doing"

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.580
"his homework right. I think
you need to put him in the"

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:24.580
"three-month program and
then you need to think about"

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:26.350
"keeping him out of school next year."

00:04:26.350 --> 00:04:27.570
That was just too quick.

00:04:27.570 --> 00:04:30.060
It just got so phony to me.

00:04:30.060 --> 00:04:31.935
And they were, like, trying to pressure me

00:04:31.935 --> 00:04:33.602
into signing him up,

00:04:33.602 --> 00:04:35.650
and he hadn't even been there a week yet.

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:36.610
So, who's ready?

00:04:36.610 --> 00:04:38.393
Garrard: Victor Sykes in the film

00:04:38.393 --> 00:04:40.010
is based off of John Smid,

00:04:40.020 --> 00:04:43.620
who ran Love in Action for many years.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:44.620
Martha: 22.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:45.453
Garrard: 22 years.

00:04:45.453 --> 00:04:47.970
And after a while, I don't
know if it's because of the

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:51.070
negative press, or if it's
because it got to his conscience,

00:04:51.070 --> 00:04:52.950
but he quit. He stepped down.

00:04:52.950 --> 00:04:55.890
And once he stepped down,
he began a slow journey

00:04:55.890 --> 00:04:59.242
of realizing that it was not
ever working, that he was

00:04:59.242 --> 00:05:00.208
selling a lie.

00:05:00.208 --> 00:05:05.091
And then, he married his now-husband,

00:05:05.099 --> 00:05:08.635
and lives in Paris,
Texas making furniture.

00:05:12.630 --> 00:05:16.760
It just did such damage to me
as well, because of the guilt

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:18.810
I feel for taking him there.

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:21.990
You do it because you think
you're saving your child,

00:05:21.990 --> 00:05:23.120
and you love your child.

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:25.810
I don't want other parents to
have to go through the guilt

00:05:25.810 --> 00:05:28.033
that I've had to go through for 14 years.

00:05:29.200 --> 00:05:32.825
Garrard: I think the
greatest value for this film

00:05:32.825 --> 00:05:34.700
is its ability to show a roadmap

00:05:34.703 --> 00:05:38.180
for people who are around LGBTQ youth

00:05:38.180 --> 00:05:42.160
to do better, and to act better,
and to become better people

00:05:42.160 --> 00:05:46.327
through it. And that's
what I hope the movie does.

